    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-human-syncope-cerebral-perfusion","source_ref":"source:research-bassetti-syncope-2014","anchor":{"section":"Abstract"},"note":"The review defines syncope as sudden brief transient loss of consciousness with postural failure due to global cerebral hypoperfusion. No event, cause, diagnosis, duration, fall, recovery, recurrence or actor state.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-human-circulatory-shock-perfusion","source_ref":"source:research-esicm-circulatory-shock-2014","anchor":{"section":"Definition, pathophysiology, features and epidemiology of shock — Definition"},"note":"Consensus defines circulatory shock as acute circulatory failure with insufficient oxygen delivery to tissue demand and cellular dysfunction. No diagnosis, type, cause, blood pressure, symptom, organ outcome, severity or treatment.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-human-pupil-light-reflex","source_ref":"source:research-openstax-ap15-central-control","anchor":{"section":"Pupillary Reflex Pathways"},"note":"Bright retinal light can trigger parasympathetic pupil constriction. No actual light, visual function, eye condition, pupil measurement, perception or actor state.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-human-heat-stress","source_ref":"source:research-cdc-niosh-heat-stress-2026","anchor":{"section":"Key Points; Overview"},"note":"CDC/NIOSH defines occupational heat stress as combined metabolic/environmental/clothing heat leading to increased body heat storage. No temperature, work, clothing, hydration, illness, injury, diagnosis, impairment or outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-human-cold-stress","source_ref":"source:research-cdc-niosh-cold-stress-2026","anchor":{"section":"Key Points; Overview"},"note":"CDC/NIOSH states extreme-cold/cold-environment exposure can cause cold stress and that heat can rapidly leave the body below normal temperature. No temperature, duration, clothing, body temperature, diagnosis, impairment, survival or treatment.","review_status":"approved"},
